# The content in this directory

The code in this repository is BSD-3-Clause. **This directory is not code and
is not under that licence.**

Every pack here is derived from sources listed in `ATTRIBUTION.md`, and some of
them are share-alike: CC BY-SA 4.0 obliges a derived work to carry the same
licence. A derived work is what a pack is — the word list, the ordering and the
glosses all come from somewhere.

So: **the JSON files under `content/echo/` are licensed CC BY-SA 4.0**, and
carry the attribution their sources require. The licence binds this directory
and nothing else: share-alike attaches to the derived content, not to the
program that reads it, and the seed script, the API and the app stay
BSD-3-Clause.

https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

Three rules follow, and each is easy to break by accident:

- **A pack file may not be moved into `packages/shared` or `apps/`.** It would
  take this licence with it into a directory that is BSD-3-Clause, and the two
  cannot both be true of one file.
- **A new source has to be recorded in `ATTRIBUTION.md` before the pack that
  uses it is committed**, with the version or the date it was taken. "CC BY-SA"
  is not attribution; a name, a link and a date are.
- **A source that is not share-alike keeps its own conditions anyway.** The
  CEFR-J wordlist is free for commercial use on one condition, that it is
  cited. Putting it into a pack that is CC BY-SA does not absorb that condition
  or make the citation optional — CC BY-SA is what *we* grant downstream, and it
  cannot be more than what we were given. Every such condition is written out in
  `ATTRIBUTION.md`, and dropping one is a licence breach in a public repo.
